\textbf{a.) How much gain in binding energy would be needed for \ce{^5{He}} to be bound in its ground state wrt disintegration into \ce{^4{He} + n}?}

The difference in binding energy between the products and reactants is positive so \ce{^4{He}} is more bound than \ce{^5{He} and energy is released}
\[B(\ce{^4{He}}) - B(\ce{^5{He}}) = 28.30-27.56=0.74\,\text{MeV}\]


\textbf{b.) How much gain in binding energy would be needed for \ce{^8{Be}} to be bound in its ground state wrt disintegration into \ce{^4{He} + ^4{He}}?}
\[2B(\ce{^4{He}}) - B(\ce{^8{Be}}) = 2(28.30)-56.50=0.1\,\text{MeV}\]


\textbf{c.) How much energy is needed to separate a neutron from \ce{^6He}?}
\[S_n = B(\ce{^6He})-B(\ce{^5He})=29.27-27.56=1.71\,\text{MeV}\]
